cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "The five-speed lonely runner spectrum below 1/5: a kernel-checked proof"
abstract: >-
  A complete formalization in Lean 4 with Mathlib of the statement that, for five distinct positive integer speeds,
  the values of the maximum loneliness ML below 1/5 are exactly s/(5s+1) with s >= 1 (the inclusion, the attainment
  of every value by (1,2,3,4,5s), and the equivalence), with no hypothesis left over and no sorry, no native_decide
  and no axiom beyond propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound. The proof is a computer-assisted case analysis
  (a tree of choices for a reduced basis of the relation lattice, two critical subtori computed by hand, and a
  finite certificate produced by Rust programs and checked by the Lean kernel), following the method of Yi, Gill
  and Bu for four speeds (arXiv:2610.40694). No first proof is claimed; whether another complete proof exists
  must be checked against the literature. The archive contains the paper, the 9,496 shipped Lean modules of a
  17,598-module import closure (the remaining regenerable generated modules are listed with sha256 and restored by a script),
  the Rust generators and a reproduction entry point (a clean rebuild of a fresh clone was run by the author on 21 and 22 November 2026: 17,598 of 17,598 modules, summed module time 40.4 hours, 27 h 39 min wall on one machine; it is not an independent rebuild). The Lean
  modules, the Rust generators and the first draft of the paper were produced by an AI system (Claude, Anthropic);
  the questions, directions and the decision to formalize this statement came from Vico Bonfioli. The paper has not
  been peer reviewed. No proof of the lonely runner conjecture is claimed. Version 1.0.1 adds the generated
  modules of eight representatives that version 1.0.0 omitted. Version 1.0.2 records the clean rebuild; the Lean
  tree is unchanged.
